{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"567b58ff-0a9f-4aae-b92b-f58f64d71dbc","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Start with a golden rectangle: its width divided by its height is phi. Removing a square leaves a smaller golden rectangle.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Squares into a spiral"},{"op":"equation","id":"ratio","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\varphi=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}\\approx1.618"},{"op":"figure","id":"nest","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.12,1.74],"y":[-0.14,1.14],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"outer","points":[[0,0],[1.618034,0],[1.618034,1],[0,1]],"closed":true},{"kind":"segment","from":[1,0],"to":[1,1]},{"kind":"measure","from":[0,-0.07],"to":[1,-0.07],"label":"1"},{"kind":"measure","from":[-0.06,0],"to":[-0.06,1],"label":"1"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"567b58ff-0a9f-4aae-b92b-f58f64d71dbc","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Keep removing the largest possible square. Each new square turns around the center and has side length divided by phi.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"divisions","on":"nest","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":[1,0.381966],"to":[1.618034,0.381966]},{"kind":"segment","from":[1.236068,0],"to":[1.236068,0.381966]},{"kind":"segment","from":[1,0.236068],"to":[1.236068,0.236068]},{"kind":"text","at":[0.5,0.52],"text":"1"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.31,0.73],"text":"0.618"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.43,0.2],"text":"0.382","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"sizes","anchor":{"el":"ratio","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"s_n=\\varphi^{-n},\\qquad n=0,1,2,\\ldots"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"By what factor does each successive square's side length shrink?","expected_gist":"Divide by phi, or multiply by about 0.618."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"567b58ff-0a9f-4aae-b92b-f58f64d71dbc","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Inside each square, draw a quarter-circle between opposite corners. These first two arcs meet with the same tangent direction.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"firstarcs","on":"nest","marks":[{"kind":"arc","center":[1,0],"r":1,"start":90,"end":180},{"kind":"arc","center":[1,0.381966],"r":0.618034,"start":0,"end":90},{"kind":"point","at":[1,1]}]},{"op":"text","id":"rule","anchor":{"el":"sizes","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"One quarter-circle per square"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"567b58ff-0a9f-4aae-b92b-f58f64d71dbc","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Continue inward through the smaller squares. This smooth chain approximates a golden spiral; the exact spiral has continuously changing curvature.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"innerarcs","on":"nest","marks":[{"kind":"arc","center":[1.236068,0.381966],"r":0.381966,"start":270,"end":360},{"kind":"arc","center":[1.236068,0.236068],"r":0.236068,"start":180,"end":270}]},{"op":"text","id":"caveat","anchor":{"el":"rule","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"Circular arcs approximate the golden spiral"},{"op":"equation","id":"scaling","anchor":{"el":"caveat","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\text{Exact spiral:}\\quad r\\!\\left(\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=\\varphi\\,r(\\theta)"}],"checkpoint":null}}
